  "answer": "### Short Answer\nIndividuals exempt from filing an income tax return under the National Internal Revenue Code (NIRC) include minimum wage earners and those whose gross sales or receipts do not exceed the VAT threshold.\n\n### Controlling Authorities\nThe relevant provisions are found in Section 24 of the NIRC, which states that minimum wage earners are exempt from income tax on their taxable income. Additionally, self-employed individuals and professionals whose gross sales or gross receipts do not exceed the VAT threshold are also exempt from filing.\n\n### Interpretation\nSection 24 of the NIRC specifies that minimum wage earners, as defined in Section 22(HH), are not required to pay income tax on their earnings. Furthermore, self-employed individuals and professionals can opt for an 8% tax rate on gross sales or receipts if their income does not exceed the VAT threshold, thus exempting them from the graduated income tax rates.\n\n### Practical Meaning\nThis means that individuals earning below the minimum wage or those whose income from self-employment or professional practice is below the VAT threshold are not obligated to file an income tax return. This exemption simplifies compliance for low-income earners and small businesses, reducing their administrative burden and tax liability.",
